            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "PRATE002"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I roast pumpkin seeds on a regular basis, and I always use this recipe now that I have found it. I must warn you: the more spice mixture you use, the better they are! The perfect balance for me is actually quadruple the mixture and still 2 cups of seeds. Also, for the salt I use 1/3 regular salt and the rest seasoned salt and they are soooooooo good! Thanks for the recipe!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Sharlene Rochelle Jones"
            },
            "reviewBody": "They turned out awesome. I tweaked the recipe a bit, didn’t have Worcestershire sauce and I used onion salt, garlic salt and a Smokey spice blend. I baked them for 30 mins in the shallow pan then I transferred them to a baking sheet for another 30 mins to make sure the roasted evenly and browned evenly. My 5 year old loves them.",
            "datePublished": "2020-10-05T22:10:48.647Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Angeliccurles"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I’ve made this several times and each time they are gone quickly. My family loves them. I rinsed and clean the pulp off the seeds and let dry over night. Well actually a few days busy schedule. Double and Used olive oil only 1/2 tsp of salt , garlic powder instead of the garlic salt to my 4 cups of seeds. Delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2022-11-17T02:35:57.114Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Finn Gypsy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Excellent cooking method and seasonings. I boiled minutes then pored into strainer and rinsed with cold water until seeds were cool. Let drip in Strainer while i made seasoning. Did 1/2 butter/veg oil and DOUBLED the rest of spice and flavoring. Followed baking instructions exactly, no issues with burning. I think because of using veg oil and NOT drying seeds.",
            "datePublished": "2020-11-06T18:50:22.870Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Erin"
            },
            "reviewBody": "So crunchy and addicting! I only had 1 1/2 cups seeds from our 2 small pumpkins, used Johnny’s instead of regular salt, and did 1/4 tsp garlic powder instead of garlic salt. I rinsed and drained seeds in a colander, then put them in preheated oven on foiled jelly roll pan to dry while I made “sauce”... about 10 min. Followed baking instructions as written. They were done in about 40 min. I just tested them each time I stirred until desired crunchiness was reached.",
            "datePublished": "2018-11-11T03:21:44.563Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ROBIN11034"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Was looking for a roasted pumpkin seed recipe. Some said to boil and dry first. I researched why you would want to do that. Results said that pumpkin seeds cook unevenly with the inside cooking faster than the shell resulting in burned insides. So ...\n\nI boiled for 10 minutes in salty water first as suggested. Mixed up the spices, butter and seeds in a bowl and spread on a cookie sheet. Stirred every 10 minutes for about 65 minutes. They were perfect. Now I'll tailor the spices/salt ratio to my taste. All in all a good recipe.",
            "datePublished": "2019-11-07T03:14:27.037Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Lorraine"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used 2 tsp seasoned salt instead of the 1/2 tsp salt, and kept everything else the same. Also, I do NOT wash off the pumpkin seeds or soak them in salt water. Instead, just pull off the pumpkin threads, but leave them covered in the pumpkin 'goo'. Gives them more of a pumpkin flavor that way. I do cook them a little slower as it helps them to dry off and break down the seeds a bit better.",
            "datePublished": "2019-11-11T02:58:18.963Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mary Beth"
            },
            "reviewBody": "The only thing that I did differently was to boil the cleaned, dried seeds in salted water, then dried before following the recipe. Spot on delish!",
            "datePublished": "2020-12-13T14:40:11.163Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Nancy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Best pumpkin seed recipe I have ever made. For some reason, mine didn’t required only about 20 minutes to roast on a cookie sheet. I doubled all seasoning and used olive oil instead of butter.",
            "datePublished": "2018-11-26T00:02:50.170Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mary Cosgrove"
            },
            "reviewBody": "They are a great idea if you don't want plain pumpkin seeds. I personally swap out the garlic salt for fresh chopped garlic though. Also, between 2 batches I have made I found that boiling the seeds in salt water for 10 minutes before baking yeilds a tastier finished seed. Watch the cook time though, mine this last time around only took about 40 minutes from start to finish before they were perfectly crispy.",
            "datePublished": "2015-10-21T00:18:49.303Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jenni"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I would highly recommend using butter or olive oil over margarine. I prefer olive oil.\n\nI'd also suggest boiling the seeds in salt water for about 10 minutes. This is supposed to keep the insides from burning while the outside is still not done. I've done this every time, and have never had the inside burn.",
            "datePublished": "2015-10-25T17:57:27.800Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "megaladon"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was fantastic! I added Lawry’s seasoned salt (about 1/4 tsp) and garlic powder (1/8 tsp) instead of garlic salt. The seasoning tastes (and smelled!) like homemade Chex Mix. Yum!",
            "datePublished": "2025-10-18T02:04:10.833Z"
          },
